Where does this stand? Should I try this out in Python (renaming/including
agrad/rev/var_stack.cpp)?

Not compiling libstan.a would simplify a tremendous number of things for the
PyStan build process.  Not only would one not have to worry about putting
libstan.a in its right place, but one could also distribute the software as
a compressed wheel which will speed up installation slightly (above and beyond
not having to compile libstan.a).

Best wishes,

-a

On 09/04, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> We also want to look at reducing model compile time.
> libstan.a isn't doing that.
>
> The only reason we build the parsers out
> as separate translation units is that Windows chokes on
> compiling it header only.  They're usable header only
> as well if the compilers get better.  I followed
> the pattern outlined in Vandevoorde and Josuttis's book:
>
>   1.  header-only file    .hpp
>   2.  definitions file    .def.hpp   (includes 1)
>   3.  instantiation file  .cpp       (includes 2)
>
> To use header only, you include (2).
>
> To use standalone, you include (1), but also
> compile (3) in its own translation unit and then link it.
>
> - Bob
>
> On Sep 4, 2014, at 4:38 PM, Marcus Brubaker <marcus.a...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> > The only reason we build libstan.a is because we need to somehow allocate the global variables for the autodiff stack which are declared in agrad/rev/var_stack.cpp.  If a model simply included that file (perhaps renaming it to var_stack_impl.hpp or something), we would no longer need libstan.a.
> >
> > Cheers,
> > Marcus
> >
> >
> >
> > On Thu, Sep 4, 2014 at 4:19 PM, Allen Riddell <a...@ariddell.org> wrote:
> > For Python at least, ensuring libstan.a is in the right place is a serious
> > challenge. Making sure it is removed and then replaced when upgrading is also
> > a concern.
> >
> > I'd welcome a header-only Stan.
> >
> > Best,
> >
> > Allen
